-**Large holdings (§ 17 EStG).** A sale of shares in any company, German or foreign, is business income if the seller held at least 1%, directly or indirectly, at any time in the five years before the sale ([§ 17(1)](https://www.gesetze-im-internet.de/estg/__17.html): "zu mindestens 1 Prozent beteiligt war"). A holding cut below 1% still counts, and gifted shares count if the giver met the test. Liquidation and capital repayments count as a sale (§ 17(4)). Under the partial-income method (Teileinkünfteverfahren), 60% of the price less 60% of cost and selling costs is taxable ([§ 3 no. 40 c](https://www.gesetze-im-internet.de/estg/__3.html), [§ 3c(2)](https://www.gesetze-im-internet.de/estg/__3c.html)), less the allowance: the share sold of EUR 9,060, cut by the gain above the same share of EUR 36,100. The result joins other income at the normal rate. A loss is 60% deductible and can offset other income, but is barred in part for shares received free or not in a qualifying holding for the full five years (§ 17(2)).
+7. On a sale, deduct the full Vorabpauschalen assessed during ownership from the gain before applying the actual partial exemption ([§ 19(1) InvStG](https://www.gesetze-im-internet.de/invstg_2018/__19.html)). Use the full assessed amount regardless of partial exemption or whether allowances prevented cash withholding, not merely its taxable fraction; do not deduct unassessed estimates. A stipulated gain of EUR 10,000 less EUR 1,120 full prior assessed Vorabpauschalen leaves EUR 8,880; a 30% equity-fund exemption then leaves EUR 6,216. Deducting only EUR 784 would wrongly leave EUR 6,451.20. Obtain actual broker history, including with a foreign broker.
+
+**Large holdings (§ 17 EStG).** A sale of shares in any company, German or foreign, is business income if the seller held at least 1%, directly or indirectly, at any time in the five years before the sale ([§ 17(1)](https://www.gesetze-im-internet.de/estg/__17.html): "zu mindestens 1 Prozent beteiligt war"). A holding cut below 1% still counts. If the seller acquired the disposed share free within the five years before disposal, § 17(1) sentence 4 applies sentence 1 correspondingly where the seller did not personally meet the test but a predecessor, or an earlier predecessor in a chain of free transfers, held the statutory participation within the relevant five-year period. Record the actual acquisition and predecessor holding dates; a free acquisition outside that recent-acquisition window does not enter this exception solely because its giver currently owns 1%. This share test is separate from AStG residence aggregation. Liquidation and capital repayments count as a sale (§ 17(4)). Under the partial-income method (Teileinkünfteverfahren), 60% of the price less 60% of cost and selling costs is taxable ([§ 3 no. 40 c](https://www.gesetze-im-internet.de/estg/__3.html), [§ 3c(2)](https://www.gesetze-im-internet.de/estg/__3c.html)), less the allowance: the share sold of EUR 9,060, cut by the gain above the same share of EUR 36,100. The result joins other income at the normal rate. A loss is 60% deductible and can offset other income, but is barred in part for shares received free or not in a qualifying holding for the full five years (§ 17(2)).
 
 - **Dividends from a large holding** stay in the flat tax by default. On application, a holder of at least 25%, or of at least 1% who works for the company with significant influence, can use the partial-income method instead ([§ 32d(2) no. 3 EStG](https://www.gesetze-im-internet.de/estg/__32d.html)). The application is made with the return at the latest, runs for four more years unless revoked, and cannot be made again for that holding after revocation. Financing costs then become 60% deductible.
 
 **Private sales (§ 23 EStG).**
 
-- Land and buildings sold "nicht mehr als zehn Jahre" after purchase; other assets (crypto, gold, foreign currency, collectibles) sold "nicht mehr als ein Jahr" after purchase ([§ 23(1)](https://www.gesetze-im-internet.de/estg/__23.html)). Exactly one year is still taxable.
+- Land and buildings sold "nicht mehr als zehn Jahre" after purchase; other assets (crypto, gold, foreign currency, collectibles) sold "nicht mehr als ein Jahr" after purchase ([§ 23(1)](https://www.gesetze-im-internet.de/estg/__23.html)). Exactly one year is still taxable. For other private assets used as an income source with income in at least one calendar year, § 23(1) no. 2 sentence 4 extends the period to ten years. The BMF's 6 March 2025 letter, paragraph 63, specifically does not apply that extension to Currency or Payment Tokens; do not extend that administrative exception to every other private asset or token classification. An independently qualifying income-producing non-token asset sold after two years is not exempt merely because one year passed.
 - Property is outside § 23 if the owner lived in it exclusively between purchase and sale, or in the year of sale and the two years before (§ 23(1) no. 1 sentence 3: "ausschließlich zu eigenen Wohnzwecken oder im Jahr der Veräußerung und in den beiden vorangegangenen Jahren"). Letting it out at any point breaks the first test; the second test looks only at the three calendar years ending with the sale. Everyday objects ("Gegenstände des täglichen Gebrauchs") are outside § 23 (§ 23(1) no. 2 sentence 2).
 - Gain = price less cost and sale costs; for a let property, cost is reduced by depreciation claimed (§ 23(3)).
 - **Freigrenze.** Gains stay tax-free if the total private-sales gain in the calendar year is "weniger als 1 000 Euro" (§ 23(3) sentence 5). It is a cliff: at EUR 1,000 or more the whole gain is taxed. The EUR 1,000 limit applies from 2024 ([Wachstumschancengesetz, BGBl. 2024 I Nr. 108](https://www.recht.bund.de/bgbl/1/2024/108/VO.html), Art. 2 no. 5, in force from 1 January 2024 under Art. 35(4)); for 2023 and earlier the gain had to be below EUR 600.
